        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress topper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the ViscoSoft 3 Inch Select High Density. ViscoSoft lists its gel memory foam at 3.1 lb/ft³, describes the feel as medium-firm, and adds corner straps and a washable cover. Side sleepers should consider the Tempur-Adapt + Cooling, and anyone with back pain should try a firm latex topper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress topper for back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A medium or firm natural latex topper about 3 inches thick, such as the Pure Green, is the best mattress topper for back pain on a supportive bed. Latex pushes back against the hips and helps keep the spine level. If pain persists for weeks, see a doctor or physical therapist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress topper for side sleepers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A soft 3-inch memory foam topper, such as the Tempur-Pedic TEMPUR-Adapt + Cooling, is the best mattress topper for side sleepers on a firm mattress. It lets the shoulder and hip sink in while the waist stays supported. Soft latex is the cooler alternative if memory foam feels too warm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 2 or 3 inch mattress topper better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 3-inch topper suits most people because it changes the feel of the bed noticeably without letting the hips sink too far. Choose 2 inches if you sleep on your back or stomach, or if your mattress is only slightly too firm. Go to 4 inches only as a side sleeper on a hard mattress.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a mattress topper fix a sagging mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A topper follows the shape of the mattress under it, so it will sink into a dip just as you do. It can soften a firm bed or slightly firm a soft one, but it cannot restore worn-out support. If your mattress has a visible dip, check its warranty and plan on replacing it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is latex or memory foam better for a mattress topper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what you want. Memory foam contours closely and relieves pressure, which side sleepers like, but it sleeps warmer and feels slow to move on. Latex is springier, cooler and usually more durable, and firm latex can add support. Latex also costs more. For back pain, it usually has the edge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I keep my mattress topper from sliding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a topper with corner straps or a grippy base, like the ViscoSoft, if sliding would bother you. For a strapless topper, use a deep-pocket fitted sheet that covers the mattress and topper together, a zippered topper cover, or a non-slip rug pad underneath, and check that the mattress itself is not sliding.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
